commentBody:
@CB
I had LAVEON.
His brother? I think? Maybe?

@Charles Peterson

You may be thinking of Le'Veon Bell, former football player. The puzzle is referring to basketball player LaMelo Ball, who does indeed have a basketball playing brother named Lonzo Ball.  Their father is LaVar Ball, and he is known for liking to be in the news. Lots of LB's.

Probably more than you wanted to know, but maybe it will help with a puzzle in the future

@Steven M.
We stayed at a hotel next to the lodge years ago. One of my best travel memories was escaping my companion’s snoring to sit at the rim listening to The Grand Canyon Suite on a cassette tape.  Any mention of that amazing place brings me right back to that moment, view and music sans snoring. 

Have a wonderful time, Steven M.

@Steven M.

I agree about the first five minutes.

But then, an hour later, when you see three separate thunderstorms happening at the same time in the same sky and a helicopter in the impossible distance seemingly barely moving among them even when reglanced ten minutes later, a different but no-less-intense wonder takes hold.

@Francis

I see the emus have finally let you out of their pen. Welcome back.
Had to wonder at first whether jungly was arboreal or psychological!

@Francis "Couldn't it just as easily be "D sharp, F, F sharp"?"
See my post, above. Basically I agree, but if you're going to be such a rococo snot to pick F-sharp major, you'd know that it was
"D-sharp, E-sharp, F-sharp."

@Al  in Pittsburgh

That sounds quite a reasonable expression of knowledge of the era.  Given that fire is an element, there's no need for quantum mechanics to explain it.

@Don H

Al in Pittsburgh and I enjoy a couple of standing topics going that pop up occasionally.  Kind of like an inside joke but  more engaging.

@Bill

😀

Ah, yes, the famous E sharp that I always forget about.  I should have known I wasn't enough of a real musician to pull off F sharp.

@sotto voce

Agree that Living was lovely.

Enjoy.

@lucky13 
100% did not know étendue, but I looked it up after. Noun (feminine) that means stretch or expanse. Related to the verb étendre which means to spread or extend or stretch. So literally "blue expanse", which is then easy. 

Having written all that, I did know étendre from Pilates class! Dang. I gotta go back to Pilates, at a minimum so I don't forget all those words.

@Becky I also have the same nit to pick about this cluing.

At least my eyebrows got a bit of a workout during the puzzle-solving part of the morning.

Remember to be kind, y’all, even when your mileage varies.  Take care!

@Ged 

