@Nancy J.

Perfect song!
I got two turntables and a microphone...

@Patrick J.

And in between, make no mistake,
That bovine cow is made of steak.

@Steve L

And when we're done with what's inside,
Don't forget to tan the hide.

@Don H

Poor beast was born to graze and chew,
Instead, he's skinned to make your shoes.

@Steve L

Lived for a while around the corner from the White Horse Tavern, where that poem was regularly recited by one patron or another, usually late into the evening, with the words slurred. Good times.

We oldsters were required to memorize poetry as elementary school students. I was assigned “The Cremation of Sam McGee,” and then in high school German class, several poems by Rilke and the opening scene of “Faust” by Goethe. I can’t remember what I had for breakfast yesterday, but I can still rattle off “Der Panther.”

@Jane Wheelaghan

If you ever got into bed and your spouse was already asleep, centrally situated so that there was room for half a person on either side, you’d know what a BED HOG is. 

Ask me how I know.

@Steve L

It took me quite a while into my marriage of hugging the edge of the mattress before I finally screwed up the courage to say, “May I have some bed, please?!”

(Now, I find a sharp elbow and “Yo!” to be more efficient.)

@Sal Z
?
A proper SCONE is not particularly sweet.

@Xword Junkie

I'd heard of Coogler, but did you know Ava Duvernay fit the squares?  :)  I had exactly the same experience as you with Co-Olympian.  My main issue with the lookout was that I assumed it meant a person or mascot rather than a place. Not that it really matters I guess.

I was sure this was a Saturday when I finished.  Hmm, XWStsts only lists it as "Hard"...

@Barry Ancona

Wonderful.


All hail the stone tablet homage to our absent Ed

@Mean Old Lady

These days I appreciate any NYT end of week that makes me sweat a little, so I enjoyed it, but I'd much rather have the difficulty level come from twisty, vague and clever cluing than from a bunch of names.  If you know them, it becomes ridiculously easy, so in my opinion, it's not a great way to fill a puzzle.

Today's Newsday puzzle took me a couple of minutes longer than this one, but proper names were rare (maybe one?).  I've been finding that to be the case with their puzzles in general, but I only do Friday and Saturday, so that might not be true for the rest of the week.

@john ezra

This is WHERE IT'S AT for an iconic song – not recognizing its words, but then the first line of the chorus comes along and you're singing it, knowing exactly how it goes and who the composer is. 
Wondrous.

@Michael
What Canadian dictionary would you recommend? 

I have an OED, but somehow these days Canadianisms feel particularly relevant.

@Francis

Aww, not an eggplant person eh!? I love it so much!! But especially fried and marinara and cheese on top. I don't like  as much when it's mushy like in some stir fries though. 

In any case, we'll always have martinis! 🍸☺️🍸

@HeathieJ
Eggplant is like okra--only good when fried.

Preferably with a coating that does not sog. (I know you like marinara on top. Does that challenge the no-sog bit? I have very strong opinions about no-sog okra coatings.)

@Lynn

For me, it's still good if the coating is crispy enough to keep the inside from getting mushy—and if one avoids over saucing it. 

That said, what we normally do at home, is fry up crispy breaded eggplant sticks and just dip them in marinara.  Very little fuss!  :-)

@HeathieJ
Excellent. I approve.

If you ever need okra tips, just ask. Although I don't have the deft touch of my brother, who is expert at removing each piece at just the right post-sog/pre-burnt point.

@Darren

But the adjective before ALES had to fill five boxes and, more importantly, had to provide the correct beginnings for five Down answers.

Especially at the end of the week, there may be many entries that will be unknown---until you seek assistance from the crosses.
